let's get into audience questions for  the week three really good questions  submit questions however you want email  Twitter Facebook YouTube Instagram  matter now first ones from mark mark  says we hear a lot about economic  inequality and the wage gap reducing it  it increasing debates over what's  actually going on but what about  answering the question why is economic  inequality actually bad yet this this is  a good question for a couple different  reasons I mean number one it's really  important for us to consider that just  because you point out that something is  unequal doesn't necessarily mean that  it's unfair and it doesn't necessarily  mean that that inequality is bad so that  this is exactly the type of question  that I think is an important one but  you've got to talk about it in good  faith right because the arguments of  freedom and liberty dictating that  whatever inequality level results in  societies is the right one because  people should be free to do or not do  whatever they want that's not a good  argument and it also implies that the  government sort of has no role in  mitigating circumstances that are  potentially bad for everybody in society  or for specific groups so let's sort of  start there when you have a high degree  of economic inequality that gives  wealthier people a higher degree of  control over the lives of others by  virtue of being able to influence the  status quo and the systems that are in  place and in the United States when you  have a Supreme Court ruling that  effectively says that money is speech  you are you are in a practical way  codifying that and saying yes  we here money is speech if you have more  money you have more speech and we have  an extraordinarily high level of  inequality which means that when we look  at our system if we want to believe that  it is a meritocracy and a true democracy  and I know people will say David we're  not a democracy were  a constitutional republic you know what  I'm talking about here we simply don't  have that when we have this degree of  inequality that's number one number two  significant degrees of economic  inequality or what we would call a  wealth gap can also undermine the  fairness of political legal and  legislative institutions and we see that  in the United States and that I would  argue is objectively bad because you  have disenfranchisement you have the  subjugation of certain groups of people  and we could talk about what groups  those are because of the degree of  inequality that we have in the United  States we have two justice systems we  talk about that all the time when it  comes to incarceration when it comes to  street crime versus white-collar crime  versus corporate crime we have all of  those things in part because of the  degree of an economic inequality that we  that we experience and then to make a  sort of societal good argument and this  resonates with some people and it  doesn't resonate with others depending  on your political outlook the negative  externalities of extreme inequality are  a malignant it's a malignant problem in  society crime is one example we know  that when you have higher degrees of  inequality you have increasing crime  even though over a long period of time  the crime rate in the United States has  has been going down so when if we say  that an interest of the government or of  society is to reduce criminality then we  should be looking at at inequality as  something that that we could focus on  now am I suggesting that we should have  equality total equality in terms of  people's wealth and people's incomes of  course not and I actually don't know  short of actual socialists and  communists and even sometimes they make  concessions on this issue I don't know  of any Social Democrats that think that  we should have a total evening or total  equality in terms of wealth but what I  would like to see  is more aggressive taxation at the very  high level in order to sort of dampen  that very very top the people who are  hoarding money to no benefit of the  economy whatsoever and a sort of raising  of the bottom that is a way to reduce  income inequality while still allowing  successful entrepreneurs and others to  have the success that they want while  improving on a lot of these metrics at  the society level and then lastly I also  want to point out that if you support  the sort of capitalist mixed economy of  some kind or even a capitalist economy  because of innovation that you believe  that it provides inequality reduces  innovation because the innovators aren't  always the actual rich people but it's  often those that they employ or people  who are not yet rich and when you have a  high degree of inequality you are dis  incentivizing those that are not already  at the top from innovating because they  they may not perceive the benefits to be  what they potentially would be in a  different scenario they may also not be  enabled or in a situation where they can  pursue that so innovation is also hurt  by inequalities er  these are a few reasons and if your  response will be why not just work on  ending poverty rather than reducing  inequality from a strictly mathematical  standpoint if everything stays the same  except everybody who's below the poverty  level is lifted to the poverty level  that is a reduction of inequality you're  effectively calling for the same thing  then it just becomes a matter sort of of  degree and once we're talking about  degrees you've already conceded that we  should seek to reduce inequality
 

